{"timestamp":"2026-08-23T18:13:02.362Z","ordinal":18034,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a02fd3-7517-7ab0-a49b-7f02c55c949d","author":"/root/phase3_quality_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/phase3_quality_review\nPayload:\nYes—this is a real HIGH preservation bug.\n\nA valid pre-existing `origin=distilled` fact may have zero provenance. Exact candidate upsert adds the v2 anchor; later `NO_FACTS` or candidate removal strips that sole anchor, and the generic cleanup deletes the record because it is distilled and now anchorless. That violates Phase 3A’s explicit legacy-preservation contract.\n\nReceipts alone cannot solve it: the facts write intentionally precedes the receipt write, so a crash in that gap leaves the anchor but no receipt. On recovery, the system must determine ownership from the facts themselves.\n\nThe durable fix needs per-anchor ownership, for example:\n\n- `candidate-application-v2-owned:<digest>` when the candidate created the fact.\n- `candidate-application-v2-shared:<digest>` when the exact fact already existed.\n\nCleanup may delete an anchorless distilled fact only after removing an owned marker. Removing a shared marker must preserve the fact. This also handles multiple candidate anchors correctly. The receipt can continue recording fact IDs, but cannot be the sole ownership authority across the crash boundary."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a02fb8-5db4-77e3-a158-975075162964"}}}
